# Resolution
Accepted as of 2026-07-07.

The reported data flow is real: DeleteBucket flight-recorder events include
the bucket name, and flight-recorder dumps can be written to stderr through
panic/500/local-debug diagnostic paths. This is accepted under the current
observability policy rather than fixed as a DeleteBucket-specific issue.

Rationale:

- Bucket names are classified by `guides/observability-formatting.md` as
  attacker-controlled text that must be escaped, not as secrets or bearer
  material that must be redacted.
- `BucketName` uses an observability-safe `Debug` implementation, so the value
  is escaped and cannot forge log structure.
- This is not specific to DeleteBucket. Many trace, flight-recorder, and
  local-debug diagnostics include escaped bucket names, object keys, upload
  IDs, or session IDs for operational debugging.
- Flight-recorder dumps are diagnostic/operator surfaces. The local-debug
  endpoint is not available in production release builds, and panic-on-500 /
  abort-on-500 are diagnostic options rather than normal production behavior.
- Request summary/error records still use path hashes where appropriate; this
  acceptance applies to explicit diagnostic flight events, not to all request
  logging.

This decision can be revisited later for operational policy reasons. If bucket
or key names need to be confidential in diagnostic dumps, the fix should be a
global observability policy change with resource-hash/redaction helpers and an
audit of all trace, flight-recorder, and local-debug event sites, rather than a
DeleteBucket-only change.

# Summary
Introduced: DeleteBucket diagnostics that were previously emitted only through opt-in tracing are now always stored in the flight recorder for request contexts, and the stored details include raw bucket names instead of hashes or redacted values.
The new observability::emit_flight_event helper persists its detail string into the global flight recorder for any request with a trace context. The converted DeleteBucket call sites pass detail strings such as bucket={:?} and include raw bucket names and some error details. Existing flight-recorder request records deliberately use path_hash and tests assert that bucket/key path components are not present, but this change bypasses that redaction for DeleteBucket. Because the flight recorder can be dumped to stderr via the panic hook, panic-on-500/abort-on-500 paths, or the local debug endpoint, private bucket names from recent DeleteBucket attempts may be disclosed to anyone with access to process logs or local debug diagnostics.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm emit_flight_event records caller-supplied detail into the flight recorder without redaction when a trace context is attached.
- [x] Confirm flight-recorder dump/panic-hook paths print record.detail to stderr verbatim.
- [x] Confirm DeleteBucket coordinator/storage call sites pass detail strings containing raw bucket names via format!("bucket={:?}", ...).
- [x] Dynamically exercise the real DeleteBucket path with a private bucket name and verify flight_recorder_snapshot contains the raw name.
- [x] Force a diagnostic dump via panic hook and verify stderr exposes the raw bucket name; attempt crash/debugger/valgrind workflow as applicable.
## Report
Validated the suspected information disclosure with a dynamic PoC against the real DeleteBucket coordinator path. Code review confirmed the data flow: crates/observability/src/lib.rs:718-728 emit_flight_event obtains the current trace context and records the supplied detail string directly with record_flight_event; crates/observability/src/lib.rs:742-762 dump_flight_recorder_to_stderr prints record.detail verbatim; crates/observability/src/lib.rs:766-770 installs that dump in the panic hook. The DeleteBucket path supplies raw bucket names to this helper, e.g. crates/server-core/src/coordinator/bucket.rs:369-379 records bucket_delete_request_start and bucket_delete_authorize_start with format!("bucket={:?}", req.name), and crates/server-core/src/coordinator/bucket.rs:423-480 records later lifecycle events with the raw name. The storage layer does the same at crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:2730-2797. I added a focused test PoC in crates/server-core/src/coordinator/bucket_tests.rs that creates bucket secret-bucket-delete-leak-poc, attaches an observability TraceContext, calls the real coord.delete_bucket path, verifies flight_recorder_snapshot contains bucket_delete_* records with the raw bucket name, installs the panic flight-recorder hook, and intentionally panics to force stderr dump. Running `cargo test -p server-core poc_delete_bucket_panic_dump_leaks_raw_bucket_name_to_flight_recorder -- --nocapture` failed intentionally and printed `POC_LEAKED_RECORDS=["bucket_delete_request_start bucket=\"secret-bucket-delete-leak-poc\"", ...]`, then the panic hook printed `== argmin flight recorder dump reason="panic" records=17 ==` followed by multiple lines such as `flight seq=1 ... target=server_core event=bucket_delete_request_start bucket="secret-bucket-delete-leak-poc"` and storage events such as `target=storage event=bucket_delete_begin_start bucket="secret-bucket-delete-leak-poc" pg_id=0`. This proves raw private bucket names are persisted and exposed in diagnostic dumps. Valgrind was attempted but is not installed in the container; memory instrumentation is not material to this logging disclosure. I also ran LLDB non-interactively and broke on `observability::dump_flight_recorder_to_stderr`; the backtrace showed frame #0 at lib.rs:743, frame #1 in the panic hook at lib.rs:770, and the PoC panic frame in bucket_tests.rs, then continuing emitted the same leaked flight-recorder records.

